picture postcard perfection  tourists are back in venice but they  won't be arriving anytime  soon on large cruise ships  it follows years of debate about banning  mega cruise ships to protect  venice's lagoon ecosystem  that debate became much more heated with  the arrival in june  of a towering 90 000 ton ship  the government finally announced the ban  in july  after threats from unesco to place  venice a world heritage site on its  endangered list  italy is promising to cover lost wages  for thousands of workers who rely on the  cruise industry to keep them afloat  we hope the government comes up with a  way for us to continue a subsidy might  last a year or two but this decree says  ships won't pass through anymore we need  certainty for our future  this is the judeka canal which cuts  right through the heart  of venice passing by saint mark's square  and it's this very artery through which  these huge ships will no longer be able  to pass  but that doesn't mean they'll be  completely blocked from the venetian  lagoon  the plan is to have large cruise ships  enter the lagoon through a back  channel and dock at the mainland  industrial port of marguera  the problem is the port isn't ready to  receive cruise passengers  city councilor marco gasparinetti grew  up along venice's canals  he welcomes the band but sees it as a  distraction  there's a visible part of the iceberg  the visible tita is the big ships but  with many other problems and the big  ships are not the only one  and what are those problems the fact  that the city is dying  in terms of being leaving a place with  citizens  living inside and not having to commute  to get a job which cannot  give you the chance to live in the city  where you're just a commuter  maeve cuchol is part of an organization  that lobbied for the ban  she sees it as part of a larger push to  focus on long-term sustainability  all of that would be done not to to  shift the focus away from  accommodating tourism to um really  fulfilling the needs of the local  residents who  depend also on the biodiversity of the  lagoon and on the  well-being of the whole environment here  venice rose is a commercial powerhouse  centuries ago to the great benefit of  its people  finding a balance that keeps coffers  full and residents from fleeing  will take more than banning cruise ships  from its waters  adam rainey al jazeera venice  unesco often adds locations to its  endangered list to draw global attention  romania's russia montana mining  landscape was made a world heritage site  last week  but it was also added to the danger list  that's because of threats to resume  mining operations in the area  in 2019 islands and protected areas in  the gulf of california were determined  to be  in danger that's largely because of the  vulnerable situation of an endangered  porpoise  made worse by illegal fishing practices  while  kenya's lake turkana national parks were  added to the danger list in 2018  after reported effects from madame in  neighboring ethiopia  well assad raymond is a spokesman for  the cop 26 coalition which advocates for  climate action  and he's joining us from london it's  good to have you with us and al jazeera  sir thank you very much indeed  let's just talk about the situation in  venice first of all why were these  ships so apparently so damaging  to the ecosystem in venice well  as your piece showed these cruise ships  are often the size of small cities  some carry over seven thousand  passengers they dump huge amounts of  sewage and other  waste into our waters they destroy  valuable marine ecology  they pollute the seas lagoons beaches  and  this industry the cruise ship industry  has literally gone unchecked  they're building bigger and bigger ships  with more and more passengers  producing more and more environmental  impacts including  the two world heritage cities such as  venice where they've damaged homes and  businesses  and of course have helped cause the  flooding that threatens that city  ultimately their only goal is profit  margins while making others pay the  price  for this pollution we often talk about  um air travel as being one of the  primary uh  emitters if you like or one of the  primary impacts on climate change  do you think the shipping around the  world is actually overlooked because  this in many ways is a  story that to those of us outside that  appears to be isolated predominantly to  to venice and and to italy but how  widespread is it  well it's very widespread so let's you  know if we think about just this last  one week  you know the floods the famines the  extreme weather impacts that we've seen  cause havoc all around the world  that's not just another warning that the  climate crisis not something that will  happen in years to come  it's a warning that it's already here  and it's deadly for many so to stop  runaway climate change and the billions  of lives and livelihoods are going to be  infected  requires few changes in this decade so  that's includes  stopping polluting industries and that's  not just fossil fuels or aviation  it's also the shipping industry look one  person on one of those cruise ships  is responsible in just one week for  producing as much emissions  as someone living in africa does over a  whole year  so you know tackling the shipping  industry tracking uh  the cruise ship industry tackling  aviation tackling fossil fuels  we fixing our food system we need to do  all of these things  because that's the scale of the  challenge that is in front of us  and unfortunately despite all of the  alarm bells we've had  lots of hot air from governments empty  promises but they aren't serious about  cutting their emissions and the  emissions that they need to cut  what matters is what we do in the next  10 years and they aren't putting in  place  policies so that people relying on those  industries exactly like the  peace the the person you had on your  piece relying on those industries for  work can be transitioned to new jobs  and they're not financing the measures  that are needed to help especially the  poorest to adapt to these crises one  stark figure that we should remember  just in this last year of the corvid  recovery  rich governments have spent something  close to 16 trillion on the corvette  recovery  but less than two percent of that has  gone to low carbon  economy measures much of it has gone to  barely night the same polluting  companies  that are causing this crisis that  continue to pollute so we really need to  have  um a real shift and a change from the  sort of economic  model and the one that says profit comes  before sustainability and becomes big  for  people in our environment this ruling  does  ban ships these large ships from the  venice lagoon but as adam was just  telling us in his report they're  basically being just redirected to  another part  of the city so everything that you've  talked about all the  the levels of emissions that these ships  are are putting out  still exist is this actually as much of  a victory as environmentalists have been  fighting for this  would have us believe well  i think with all of these challenges we  you've often it's a david and goliath  situation who have local communities  local people fighting decades-long  struggles to raise  issues and see these as the first step  so  of course this doesn't solve the real  problem of course it doesn't go far  enough  it's a first step and i think many  people would see it as as  literally only the first step really the  solution is going to be to ask ourselves  in this world that we live in now can we  afford  to have these mega cruise ships where  people  are sailing around the world causing  huge environmental impacts and damages  can we afford to have these polluting  industries continuing the way that they  are  and frankly the answer is no so  accommodating them  as venice has done to try and build a  new harbor that's just shifting the  problem  